WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.344 --> 00:00:08.810
JULIA ELLIOTT: So
actually, show of hands,

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:11.270
how many people have
heard of Kaggle?

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:12.320
OK, great.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.510
How many have actually
participated in a competition

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.520
or done something on Kaggle?

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.620
OK, fewer hands, OK.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:22.670
So Kaggle is the world's
largest data science community.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:25.610
We are nearly 2 million, as
you might have caught wind

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:28.550
of in the keynote yesterday.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.490
We span the globe.

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:38.005
So we have people coming from
all regions across the globe.

00:00:38.005 --> 00:00:42.410
The community consists of people
from a variety of backgrounds,

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:43.970
not just data science
practitioners,

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:49.970
but hobbyists, researchers,
academics, and even software

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:54.290
engineers, and people
from all backgrounds.

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:56.690
So why are people
coming to Kaggle?

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:58.760
Why are there 2 million users?

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:03.110
Predominantly, we hear
it's for learning, sharing,

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:04.879
and practicing data science.

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:09.200
And they do that by
engaging with three products

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.250
on our platforms.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.910
The first being
our competitions--

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:15.770
so this is what Kaggle
has traditionally

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:17.480
been most well-known for.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.050
This is where companies
and organizations

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:24.120
host machine-learning problems.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.870
Second is our
Kernels environment.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:31.610
So this is our cloud-based
data science workbench.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:37.550
People collaborate and
create code and notebooks

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:41.030
using their datasets.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:44.010
And that brings me to our third
product which is Datasets.

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.340
So this is our
open data platform.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:51.830
Currently, we're hosting about
nearly 9,000 public datasets

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:56.870
that people have uploaded to
share and use within their data

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.820
science projects.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:02.720
So today, of course, because
I'm with the competitions team,

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.910
I am going to focus
on competitions.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:10.130
And so what is a
Kaggle competition?

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:13.850
So Kaggle hosts supervised
machine-learning competitions.

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:18.470
And at any given time, you can
visit our competitions page

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:19.970
to see what is live.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:23.340
This is actually a snapshot
from several months ago.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.580
But I think it
gives a nice glimpse

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:28.010
at the variety and the
types of competitions

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:36.200
that we host from image
classification to forecasting.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.480
And then, this over
here on the right,

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:42.440
is a snapshot of a
competition that we

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.220
had run with Porto Seguro
which is a Brazilian insurance

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:47.330
company.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:50.720
Insurance companies
commonly run their use cases

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.730
in this type of an environment.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:55.370
And so they were looking to
predict if a driver would

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.670
file a claim in the next year.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:01.520
And so I'll use
Porto as an example

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.040
of what a competition is
and how it's structured.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.700
So using that as an
example, the setup

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:14.390
is quite simple for a supervised
machine-learning competition.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:17.000
We work with the
host who provides

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:22.610
a large labeled dataset with
a target variable to predict.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:24.440
We work with our
data science team

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:27.710
to cleanse and prepare the
dataset for the platform.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:31.100
And then we split it into a
training set and a test set.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:33.650
So the training set
is what we provide

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:38.330
to competitors with the
labels or ground truth.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:40.550
And that's what they
train their algorithms on

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:43.250
to generate predictions
against a test set.

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:47.660
And that test set is what we
use to evaluate and assess

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:53.600
the accuracy of the algorithms
that are being submitted.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.500
And so using Porto
as an example,

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:00.170
if I grossly oversimplify
the data set,

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:02.070
perhaps they provided
training data,

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:06.500
including metadata on
drivers' age and income--

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:10.430
de-identified and anonymized
of course and then, of course,

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:12.290
the ground truth labels
of whether or not

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:13.940
the claim was filed.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:16.279
So our competitors
join the competition,

00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:19.980
they download the data,
they build their algorithms

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.920
and then submit predictions
against the test

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:28.010
set, where the target variables'
answers have been withheld.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:32.420
We score the accuracy
of those predictions

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:37.700
based on an evaluation metric
and then rank those competitors

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:39.730
on a live leaderboard.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:42.200
And so the
leaderboard ultimately

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:47.930
is what results in who
receives prize money sponsored

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:51.200
by the host of the competition.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.920
And it's this
competitive dynamic

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:59.900
that really fuels
people's eagerness

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:03.770
to achieve and outpace one
another throughout the duration

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:04.640
of the competition.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:08.550
And it's ultimately something
that we witness time and again

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:13.917
through looking at the
score progression over time

00:05:13.917 --> 00:05:15.000
for any given competition.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:22.240
So for Porto Seguro, we saw
that, over time, the score,

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.400
the highest leaderboard score,
converged on this asymptote

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.070
or what we consider to
be the maximum of what

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:33.790
is achievable on that
data set and that problem.

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:36.990
So now that I've
talked a little bit

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:42.060
about how the competitions
are set up and structured,

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:44.880
I want to bring that to life a
little bit and talk about some

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.970
of the common use
cases that we witness

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:51.080
customers running on Kaggle.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:54.150
And so I'll talk about four
different use cases and then

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:58.080
how the businesses are gaining
value through running a Kaggle

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:59.560
competition.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:02.430
So the first is
sales forecasting.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:08.580
So Corporacion Favorita is
a leading Ecuadorean grocer.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:12.360
They were looking to
better predict and forecast

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:15.250
sales of items in their stores.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:17.850
So this is a classic
forecasting problem.

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:21.910
As you might imagine, retailers
need to get this right.

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:22.920
It affects inventory.

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:25.890
It affects staffing--
and especially

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:28.620
when you're dealing with
perishable items, where

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:34.840
the cost of shelf life
is even more important.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:41.220
So Favorita provided a
series of very rich datasets

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:43.590
that included things
like store and item

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:48.090
metadata, promotional
activities, holidays and events

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:50.905
in Ecuador, even oil prices.

00:06:50.905 --> 00:06:52.530
And then they threw
it at the community

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:57.360
and said, what can you
do to predict unit sales?

00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:59.879
And of course, at the
end of the competition,

00:06:59.879 --> 00:07:01.920
they were able to receive
the winning algorithms.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:04.290
But one thing that
we actually find,

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:08.260
perhaps even more valuable
than the winning algorithms,

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:11.670
is actually the activity
that happens on our forums.

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:14.220
And so for every
competition, there

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:18.750
is a discussion
and a forum where

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:24.330
people are able to share ideas,
approaches that they're taking,

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:25.966
methods, techniques.

00:07:25.966 --> 00:07:27.840
And at the end of the
competition, of course,

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:29.700
is their solutions.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:31.470
So for this type
of a competition,

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:34.590
where it's a feature-rich
tabular dataset,

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.770
you saw people doing some
creative and clever feature

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:40.020
engineering.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:44.100
But one revelation that
came about at the end

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.890
of the competition
was the discovery

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:51.240
of the use of WaveNet for
this type of a problem.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:55.530
So WaveNet was
conceived by DeepMind,

00:07:55.530 --> 00:08:01.500
traditionally was used for
taking raw audio waveforms

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:04.170
and then generating speech.

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:07.530
But competitors
found that applying

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:11.700
that type of convolutional
neural network, the WaveNet,

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:16.960
was actually a great
predictor of time series data.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.500
And so they applied
it on this problem.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:23.310
And many of the leading teams
were able to make advancements

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:25.150
in this regard.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:31.890
So I'm using this as an example
to illustrate how people often

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:38.370
share breakthrough findings,
new packages, novel approaches

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:39.690
through Kaggle.

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:42.330
And I would even argue that,
sometimes, you can even

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:45.750
track the proliferation
of different types

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:49.420
of these new concepts through
their frequency and appearance

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:51.210
within Kaggle's forums.

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:53.800
So that's our first example.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:55.890
Secondly, recommender
systems-- so

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:59.700
recommender systems,
very commonly approached

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:01.170
in machine learning.

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:07.890
For Airbnb, they were looking to
predict where a new user would

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:09.960
make their first booking.

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:15.390
And so the reason, actually,
why I'm using this example--

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:18.540
it wasn't necessarily for Airbnb
about the solutions, but really

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:22.320
about the approaches
and the people who

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:23.880
were attacking their problem.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:27.990
And that was because they came
to us running a recruiting

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:29.550
competition.

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:33.780
And so what we find is that
people in our community, as

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:35.040
well as customers--

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:38.070
whether it's through
competitions, or jobs board,

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:39.600
or just forum chatter--

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:42.390
are really looking to raise
their profile within the data

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:43.170
science community.

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:47.820
And so we have countless
stories of people finding

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:51.330
their next job through
sharing progress

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:53.850
that they've made on Kaggle
or using their data science

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:58.110
projects as examples
in interviews.

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:00.740
And so the Airbnb
example is really

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:05.060
about a recruiting motivation.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:10.970
Thirdly, I thought I'd touch
on another common and rapidly

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:12.960
rising use case in
machine learning,

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:15.870
which is natural
language processing.

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:18.600
So of course, with the advent
of things like Google Home

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:20.630
and Assistant,
we're seeing a lot

00:10:20.630 --> 00:10:26.220
of Natural Language Processing
interest on our platform.

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:30.830
So Google's own Jigsaw team
was hosting a competition

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:36.380
to help identify and classify
toxic comments into, actually,

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:40.610
six different subclasses--
things like threats, hate,

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:44.000
obscenities, that sort of thing.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:49.860
And their mission was to
really identify and almost get

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:52.310
ahead of negative
behaviors in order

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:55.520
to promote better online
conversations-- so a really

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:58.040
nice noble mission.

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:02.480
And the Jigsaw team
was actually very

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:05.090
interested in the
different approaches

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:09.330
that people were taking on
this newer area of study.

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:12.830
And the things that you
might expect out of NLP

00:11:12.830 --> 00:11:17.110
like playing around with
embeddings, or language

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:20.120
normalization
preprocessing techniques,

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:21.850
neural networks of
course, and just

00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:24.080
throwing as much as
they can at a problem.

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:27.080
And that's actually
something that we commonly

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:30.140
see in Kaggle competitions.

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:32.000
We call it a
kitchen sink method,

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:34.700
which is all the different
techniques that you

00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:36.980
can throw at a problem--

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:41.780
what sticks, what works best,
what advances the problem more.

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:47.150
And as the title of
my session suggests,

00:11:47.150 --> 00:11:50.390
when you have this largest
audience of community

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:54.800
members attacking a problem with
these brute force techniques,

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:57.180
you're bound to find
something that works.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:12:02.820
And that's kind of how
problems like this advance.

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:07.370
And so one thing that
we see evidence of that

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:09.870
through our Kernels.

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:12.770
So for every
competition, there's

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:14.270
a discussion or a
forum, but there's

00:12:14.270 --> 00:12:17.690
also a series of Kernels
that people have shared--

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:22.280
so that's code and notebooks
with, oftentimes, baseline

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:23.600
models.

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:26.600
And in this particular
competition,

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:30.500
there was actually quite
a lot of Kernels shared.

00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:33.380
And the winning
algorithms tended

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:36.710
to be some blend of the
best techniques that

00:12:36.710 --> 00:12:40.045
surfaced to the top.

00:12:40.045 --> 00:12:42.420
Just an interesting anecdote
about this competition

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:45.020
is we did some
post-competition analysis

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:49.130
and discovered that any
algorithm that performed quite

00:12:49.130 --> 00:12:53.510
well on one subtype, one of
those subclasses of toxicity,

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:57.150
actually performed quite well
across all of the subtypes.

00:12:57.150 --> 00:13:00.290
So there wasn't necessarily
a specialist algorithm.

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:02.380
They performed well
across all subclasses,

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:05.310
which was quite interesting.

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:08.720
So the last area
that is, of course,

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:10.970
quite hot in machine learning
is image classification,

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:13.340
computer vision.

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:17.060
To date, our highest
prize pool competition

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:22.130
was hosted by the Department
of Homeland Security, TSA.

00:13:22.130 --> 00:13:26.420
And so if you flew here,
or if you've ever flown,

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:29.210
you probably run
into these scanners.

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:32.450
And so what the
TSA is trying to do

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:40.220
is, of course, detect objects
that are cause for threats

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:43.760
and then cause for
secondary screening.

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:50.430
But of course, when people are
alarmed for non-threat objects,

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:54.890
so falsely alarmed, that
wastes people's time,

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:57.800
it causes stress, Sometimes,
people miss flights.

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:04.220
And so the TSA is trying to curb
those kind of false positives,

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:08.000
while at the same time, not
risking passenger safety

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:10.850
by missing real threats.

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:16.190
And so they provided a
really nice test dataset

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:23.510
of volunteers' scans
to mimic threat objects

00:14:23.510 --> 00:14:27.020
and then had people
identify whether or not

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:31.640
there was an object in each of
the zones of a person's body.

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:34.400
And the images that
they used were actually

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:37.460
quite complex and large.

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:38.930
There were two
different formats.

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:40.700
There was a 3D format.

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:46.370
And then there was also a
2D cross-sectional format.

00:14:46.370 --> 00:14:51.144
And rather than try to explain
to you the winning algorithms

00:14:51.144 --> 00:14:53.310
and the techniques that
were used, which, of course,

00:14:53.310 --> 00:14:56.940
you can go to our forums
and find out more about,

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:59.010
I thought I would use
this as an opportunity

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:02.460
to share the walks of life
and the diversity in the walks

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:04.980
of life that people
who win or participate

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:07.140
in these competitions come from.

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:11.850
So of course, you're
going to have your PhDs

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:15.492
or advanced degree
participants who specialize

00:15:15.492 --> 00:15:16.950
in medical imaging,
have many years

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:19.590
of working with computer vision.

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:25.410
But interesting anecdote, within
our 10 winners, one of them

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:28.860
was actually a UC
Berkeley freshmen.

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:34.140
Another was someone who is
a data science practitioner,

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:36.390
but actually never
touched computer vision

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:40.560
and just wanted to work with
something really cool and foray

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:43.410
into computer vision.

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:47.130
And then lastly, we had a team
of just Kaggle enthusiasts.

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:50.340
They were data
science practitioners.

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:52.440
But they made-- I
don't want to say

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:55.920
a career-- but a hobby of
working on Kaggle competitions

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:58.230
and taking learnings from
their prior competitions

00:15:58.230 --> 00:16:01.680
to feed into this
particular win.

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:05.880
And so we see that, from
across all our competitions,

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:09.000
across all our
participants, everyone

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:13.740
is converging to really share
and learn from one another

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:16.950
and especially in trending
areas like computer vision

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:20.940
where advanced techniques
might need to be employed.

00:16:25.179 --> 00:16:27.101
That kind of got mixed up.

00:16:27.101 --> 00:16:27.600
Ooh.

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:28.100
Sorry.

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:35.430
So I covered a few of
the classic use cases

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:37.980
for Kaggle competitions.

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:41.760
What I thought I'd talk about
now is where we're headed.

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:45.960
So where I'll start
with that story

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:49.330
is actually Kaggle's
broader vision.

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:52.380
So if you've come
to Kaggle lately,

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:54.510
and you're familiar
with our competitions,

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:56.910
you'll see that we've
introduced these other product

00:16:56.910 --> 00:16:58.826
areas that I've touched
on previously, Kernels

00:16:58.826 --> 00:16:59.670
and Datasets.

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:02.960
And so what we envision
to be is a home

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:06.240
for data science, a place where
people are not just playing,

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:09.780
but actually doing their
data science projects,

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:11.190
doing their work.

00:17:11.190 --> 00:17:15.290
And we see that
happening by offering

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:19.109
a combination of a
rich community that's

00:17:19.109 --> 00:17:22.859
ready to share and
learn, datasets

00:17:22.859 --> 00:17:28.050
where you can do your work,
and then an environment where

00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:32.130
people can create code,
reproducible code,

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:35.550
and collaborate on that code.

00:17:35.550 --> 00:17:41.730
So we see competitions
moving in this direction

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:44.250
as well to leverage these
strengths that we are

00:17:44.250 --> 00:17:47.290
building in a more genuine way.

00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:50.640
And so where we're going to
do is towards this concept

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:51.940
of code competitions.

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:54.900
And so today, the
way I described

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:57.000
the way competitions
are set up today,

00:17:57.000 --> 00:18:02.220
people are actually
submitting predictions

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:04.020
that their model is
generating-- models

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:06.000
that they're building
probably locally, maybe

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:08.350
in some cloud environments.

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:13.080
But what we envision is
that people can actually

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:13.920
submit their code.

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:16.980
And so we've run a
handful of these so far.

00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:20.590
And we're building towards
offering more of these.

00:18:20.590 --> 00:18:22.980
But an example of
this is a competition

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:25.950
that we ran hosted by Mercari.

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:32.520
And so they are an online
marketplace where people

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:35.130
sell their secondhand goods.

00:18:35.130 --> 00:18:37.590
And so they were
looking to improve

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:39.930
the prediction of prices
that were suggested

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:42.640
to the sellers of those goods.

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:45.330
So what they do is they submit
their code within our Kaggle

00:18:45.330 --> 00:18:47.532
Kernels environment.

00:18:47.532 --> 00:18:48.990
They can collaborate
with teammates

00:18:48.990 --> 00:18:52.800
by sharing that code with
those teammates privately.

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:55.680
And then, they submit the
actual code rather than just

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:57.900
the predictions to that code.

00:18:57.900 --> 00:19:02.040
So what you can imagine
is that, for the customers

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:08.940
in the equation, they're
receiving the code that

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:13.620
was used to generate the
submissions in a more

00:19:13.620 --> 00:19:16.140
reproducible environment.

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:23.220
They can also run that code
on a truly held-out test set.

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:25.830
And then, for our
community, they're

00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:30.300
able to work in an environment
that enables them to truly

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:33.520
collaborate with one another.

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:37.110
And so this is all moving
towards our goal of really

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:40.170
being a place where
people can share and learn

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:44.280
from one another by creating an
environment for them to do so.

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:49.350
And so that is our
vision for where

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:53.250
we're headed in competitions.

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:57.290
So what I'd like to do
now is actually step you

00:19:57.290 --> 00:20:01.130
through a live example
of a competition.

00:20:01.130 --> 00:20:05.570
And so I'll start
my demo portion.

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:10.130
So this is our
Competition's listing page.

00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:12.050
Any user-- you sign
up for an account,

00:20:12.050 --> 00:20:15.130
you can get access to this.

00:20:15.130 --> 00:20:17.400
What I thought I'd
do is just show you.

00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:19.140
So this is just today, right--

00:20:19.140 --> 00:20:21.560
the active competitions
we have going on.

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:23.780
We have a series of
featured competitions,

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:25.700
and these are the ones
that are typically

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:30.890
hosted by commercial
organizations or companies who

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:36.560
are looking to receive
the solutions for each

00:20:36.560 --> 00:20:38.090
of the challenges.

00:20:38.090 --> 00:20:41.750
They post what their
prize pool is as well

00:20:41.750 --> 00:20:45.890
as the summary of what
they're trying to solve.

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:48.140
Below those, we have
a series of Getting

00:20:48.140 --> 00:20:53.570
Started Competitions-- so
your classic MNIST and Titanic

00:20:53.570 --> 00:20:54.230
competitions.

00:20:54.230 --> 00:20:56.780
We refresh these so people
can continue to work on those

00:20:56.780 --> 00:20:58.995
and learn, especially if
you're getting started.

00:20:58.995 --> 00:21:01.370
And then, followed by a series
of research and playground

00:21:01.370 --> 00:21:04.400
competitions-- so you
can see that a series

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:07.520
of these playground competitions
are using our new format

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:10.250
of code competitions.

00:21:10.250 --> 00:21:12.890
So what I thought I'd do
is actually jump into--

00:21:12.890 --> 00:21:15.830
this isn't necessarily
a featured competition.

00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:20.460
But it's a competition we
launched about a week ago.

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:24.620
And it's being hosted by the
Inter-American Development

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:25.460
Bank.

00:21:25.460 --> 00:21:30.710
And they are trying to improve
the prediction of poverty

00:21:30.710 --> 00:21:34.310
levels within a country.

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:36.200
So in this case,
it's Costa Rica.

00:21:36.200 --> 00:21:39.470
And they're doing
this because they

00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:45.910
use that as a way to
assign social welfare need

00:21:45.910 --> 00:21:48.574
and these types of programs--

00:21:48.574 --> 00:21:50.580
when it loads.

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:51.080
OK.

00:21:51.080 --> 00:21:55.880
So this is the live
competition site.

00:21:55.880 --> 00:21:58.820
It gives a little
background of what

00:21:58.820 --> 00:22:01.400
that host is trying to achieve.

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:03.470
It gives an overview
of the evaluation

00:22:03.470 --> 00:22:06.020
metric we're using to
score the submissions.

00:22:06.020 --> 00:22:09.080
And so we have a library of
these evaluation metrics.

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:11.210
And we work with the host
to identify what they're

00:22:11.210 --> 00:22:12.584
trying to emphasize,
what they're

00:22:12.584 --> 00:22:15.290
trying to de-emphasize in
order to come to an evaluation

00:22:15.290 --> 00:22:18.440
metric that makes the most
sense for that problem.

00:22:18.440 --> 00:22:19.520
We have prizes.

00:22:19.520 --> 00:22:24.200
In this case, it's
just Kaggle swag.

00:22:24.200 --> 00:22:26.420
This is considered a
for-good challenge,

00:22:26.420 --> 00:22:30.160
and therefore, we're not
looking to award prize money.

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:33.740
And then timeline associated
at any important dates--

00:22:33.740 --> 00:22:39.751
the data itself is accessible
through the competition's data

00:22:39.751 --> 00:22:40.250
page.

00:22:40.250 --> 00:22:44.699
And we've added
some new features.

00:22:44.699 --> 00:22:46.490
The data, of course,
can only be downloaded

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:50.150
after you've accepted
the competition's rules.

00:22:50.150 --> 00:22:53.330
And we have added a
new feature down here

00:22:53.330 --> 00:22:56.240
around what we
call DataExplorer.

00:22:56.240 --> 00:23:02.300
And it gives people a glimpse
at what the data for a given

00:23:02.300 --> 00:23:04.780
dataset looks like.

00:23:04.780 --> 00:23:06.680
And so you can
download that data

00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:10.160
once you have accepted the
rules for the competition.

00:23:10.160 --> 00:23:13.580
And then, you can use
it to train or test

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:15.770
your algorithm's
performance locally

00:23:15.770 --> 00:23:19.190
or in a cloud environment.

00:23:19.190 --> 00:23:23.110
As I mentioned before, there
are Kernels for the competition.

00:23:23.110 --> 00:23:26.690
So these are completely
organically generated

00:23:26.690 --> 00:23:28.370
by our community.

00:23:28.370 --> 00:23:32.240
Oftentimes, they range from
exploratory data analyses--

00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:38.030
so people looking at the
data and what features

00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:40.460
appear to have some importance.

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:42.800
And both share
their notebooks that

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:46.370
do that type of
exploration and plots.

00:23:46.370 --> 00:23:52.070
And then, within Kernels also
is, of course, some often basic

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:55.110
baseline models as well.

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:59.360
Our discussion pages
is where people

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:04.310
share anecdotes about the
approaches that they're taking,

00:24:04.310 --> 00:24:08.330
the types of features that
they're trying to explore,

00:24:08.330 --> 00:24:10.190
if they want to team
up on a competition,

00:24:10.190 --> 00:24:12.690
and that sort of thing.

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:16.850
And then, the leaderboard is,
of course, the ongoing tally

00:24:16.850 --> 00:24:18.300
of how people are performing.

00:24:18.300 --> 00:24:21.690
Competitions typically
last two to three months.

00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:23.810
And at the end of
the competition,

00:24:23.810 --> 00:24:30.740
those in the winning spots
will receive the prizes.

00:24:30.740 --> 00:24:34.550
The other thing that I thought
I'd share in this context

00:24:34.550 --> 00:24:39.710
is an actual Kernel model.

00:24:39.710 --> 00:24:42.530
So I'll do that, actually,
through another competition

00:24:42.530 --> 00:24:44.949
that I can highlight we just
actually launched yesterday

00:24:44.949 --> 00:24:47.240
because I know that we have
a starter Kernel that we've

00:24:47.240 --> 00:24:48.570
created.

00:24:48.570 --> 00:24:50.300
So for this particular
competition,

00:24:50.300 --> 00:24:55.460
it's using, actually, a
public BigQuery dataset--

00:24:55.460 --> 00:25:01.730
New York City Taxi data, which
includes the origination point

00:25:01.730 --> 00:25:04.730
as well as the end point for a
bunch of different taxi fares

00:25:04.730 --> 00:25:08.930
and then the corresponding
fare for that ride.

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:13.460
And so the idea is
to create models

00:25:13.460 --> 00:25:18.490
that will predict what the fare
will be based on that data.

00:25:18.490 --> 00:25:21.680
And so we just launched this
this morning, or last night.

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:24.830
And so we're still
gaining some teams here.

00:25:24.830 --> 00:25:32.270
But we did create a Kernel that
uses a simple linear model.

00:25:32.270 --> 00:25:34.190
And so you can see
what one of these

00:25:34.190 --> 00:25:37.370
looks like that actually
generates a solution.

00:25:37.370 --> 00:25:40.610
What he's done is he's
done a very basic look

00:25:40.610 --> 00:25:43.880
at the distance
between the two points,

00:25:43.880 --> 00:25:45.920
between the start and
end points and then

00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:51.050
taking a look at using that as a
way to generate a model for how

00:25:51.050 --> 00:25:52.760
to predict the fare.

00:25:52.760 --> 00:25:55.910
And this type of a very
basic approach, I think,

00:25:55.910 --> 00:25:58.015
gets him an RMSE value of--

00:25:58.015 --> 00:25:59.720
so that's Root
Mean Squared Error,

00:25:59.720 --> 00:26:02.510
which is the evaluation
metric used in this case.

00:26:02.510 --> 00:26:06.020
So it'll get that RMSE value
of maybe around $5 or $6.

00:26:06.020 --> 00:26:10.940
But what the competition
is hoping to help people do

00:26:10.940 --> 00:26:14.360
is make advancements
using other techniques.

00:26:14.360 --> 00:26:16.670
And actually, this was
hosted in coordination

00:26:16.670 --> 00:26:20.810
with Coursera who is
offering a course that's

00:26:20.810 --> 00:26:25.800
helping people use TensorFlow
to work on this type of problem.

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:31.100
And so what this does is it
generates an output submission

00:26:31.100 --> 00:26:38.810
that can be used to be submitted
directly to a competition.

00:26:38.810 --> 00:26:41.570
For sake of quickness,
I've gone ahead

00:26:41.570 --> 00:26:50.510
and forked that same Kernel and
rerun it, commit it in order

00:26:50.510 --> 00:26:56.150
to be able to show how you might
take the output of the Kernel

00:26:56.150 --> 00:27:00.110
and then submit it to a
competition for evaluation.

00:27:00.110 --> 00:27:04.220
And then, what that does is
that immediately generates

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:08.000
your score and then places
you on the leaderboard

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:10.502
for that competition.

00:27:13.280 --> 00:27:19.030
So that's the summary of
how a competition works,

00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:21.730
what we're doing, different
types of use cases

00:27:21.730 --> 00:27:24.050
that we're solving
for customers.

00:27:24.050 --> 00:27:24.980
OK.

00:27:24.980 --> 00:27:26.230
Well, thank you all very much.

00:27:26.230 --> 00:27:26.930
Have a good day.

00:27:26.930 --> 00:27:27.430
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:27.430 --> 00:27:30.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

